.shell
  .page-hero.reveal
    .page-hero__mark.sc-mark
      %img{src: asset_path('sc24_logo_2.png'), alt: 'SC24', fetchpriority: "high", decoding: "async"}
    .page-hero__body
      %h1.page-hero__title Team UNM · SC24
      %p.page-hero__sub HPC Student Cluster Competition
      %p.page-hero__meta Atlanta, GA · November 2024
      .page-hero__links
        - sc24_links.each do |label, href|
          %a.page-hero__link{href: href, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener"}
            = icon("arrow-out")
            = label

-# ── 01 · Cluster configuration ────────────────────────────────
.shell.section

  .sc-duo
    .sc-figure.reveal
      %img{src: asset_path('SC24-03.png'), alt: 'Cluster diagram', loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"}

    .spec.reveal
      .spec__head
        %span Per Node
      - sc24_cluster.each do |label, value|
        .spec__row
          %span.spec__label= label
          %span.spec__value= value

-# ── 02 · H100 power performance ───────────────────────────────
-# The measured argument for the configuration above, so it sits directly
-# under it. These are diagrams, not photographs: the axis labels have to
-# read at rest, so the carousel takes the whole content column at the
-# charts' own 3:2 rather than a half-width slot, and the commentary
-# stacks underneath instead of beside.
.shell.section

  .sc-charts
    .reveal
      -# --contain letterboxes them so no axis is ever cropped away, which
      -# is exactly why these stay a carousel rather than joining the
      -# mosaic below. They still open in the viewer.
      .gallery.gallery--contain.gallery--chart{data: {autoplay: 45000, frames: true, plate: "H100 power performance"}, "data-frame-key" => "h100", role: "region", "aria-roledescription" => "carousel", "aria-label" => "H100 power performance charts"}
        .gallery__bar
          - sc24_charts.each_with_index do |_, n|
            %button.gallery__seg{type: "button", "aria-label" => "Chart #{n + 1}"}
              %span.gallery__seg-fill
        .gallery__viewport{tabindex: "0"}
          - sc24_charts.each do |file, alt|
            .gallery__slide{data: {frame: true, full: viewer_src(file)}, "data-frame-id" => File.basename(file, ".*")}
              %img{src: asset_path(file), alt: alt, loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"}
        %button.gallery__arrow.gallery__arrow--prev{type: "button", "aria-label" => "Previous"}
          = icon("arrow-left")
        %button.gallery__arrow.gallery__arrow--next{type: "button", "aria-label" => "Next"}
          = icon("arrow-right")
        %span.gallery__count

    .prose.reveal
      %p
        Over the summer leading up to the competition I ran benchmarks on a single H100 GPU using the HPL benchmark — both the Nvidia optimized benchmarks container and my own pre-optimized HPL parameter set — to mirror the environment we planned to use in competition.
      %p
        Setting the TDP to 100% proved to be the
        %strong most inefficient
        power use case. I expected efficiency to peak around 75–80% TDP, but was surprised to find it continue to increase linearly all the way down to
        %strong 50% TDP.
      %p
        A rough estimate placed our cluster at 350W × 2 sockets × 3 nodes = 2,100W CPU, and 400W × 4 GPUs × 3 nodes = 4,800W GPU — ~6,900W total ignoring memory and other components. Based on the applications for this year's competition, we had room to dynamically allocate resources per workload while staying under the power cap. We chose a configuration with iDRAC chips, enabling us to power nodes on and off remotely without physically touching the cluster — a competition requirement after the benchmarking phase.

-# ── 03 · Team poster ──────────────────────────────────────────
.shell.section

  .sc-plate.sc-plate--wide.reveal
    %button.disclosure__trigger.sc-toggle{type: "button", data: {disclosure: true}, "aria-expanded" => "false", "aria-controls" => "sc24-poster"}
      View Team Poster
      = icon("chevron-down")
    #sc24-poster.disclosure__panel
      %div
        .sc-poster
          .sc-poster__sheet
            %img{src: asset_path('SC24Poster.jpg'), alt: 'SC24 Poster', loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"}
          %a.btn{href: sc24_poster_pdf, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener"}
            = icon("pdf")
            View PDF

-# ── 04 · Photographs ──────────────────────────────────────────
-# One pool, not four labelled sets: every frame of the competition, the
-# team, the shipping and the build is on the page at once, cut by the
-# same packer the home page uses. .bleed rather than .shell — an
-# unlabelled sheet has nothing to align to a text column, so it takes the
-# whole width and keeps only the gutter, exactly as the home page's own
-# sheets do.
.bleed.section.reveal

  = render "mosaic", plate: sc24_pool, key: "sc24"

= render "viewer"
